{
  "esm": "1.0.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "levelset_weno5_eno1_2d_bump_mms",
    "description": "Method-of-manufactured-solutions order-of-accuracy driver for grids/cartesian_uniform_2d/rules/weno5_eno1_norm_D2_extrapolate_bc (NCAR fire_behavior's default hybrid WENO5/ENO1 level-set gradient norm, fire_upwinding = 9) on the unit box with the linear-extrapolation halo closure. OPERATOR MMS in accumulator form: the smooth manufactured field g = (x-1/2)^2 + (y-1/2)^2 - 0.09 + eps b, b = (max(0, 1 - r^2/w^2))^8 (a C^7 bump of width w = 0.28 and amplitude eps = 0.05 on a paraboloid whose zero level set is the circle r = 0.3) is a constant observed u = g (not a state) and the single state w with w(0) = g integrates w_t = S (|grad g| - |grad u|_h), so w(T) - g = -T S (|grad g|_h - |grad g|) is EXACTLY T times the truncation error of the hybrid norm on g -- the same quantity the coupled forced-steady form u_t = S (|grad g| - |grad u|_h) measures to leading order, without its feedback. The coupled form is used by the Godunov siblings (rules/godunov_norm_D2_*, rules/godunov_upwind_D1*), whose Hamiltonians are monotone; it is NOT used here because the hybrid scheme's ENO1 branch (minmod of the two one-sided differences) is not an upwind discretisation for the transported error e_t = -S n . grad e: in the coupled form the wall-band error grows erratically instead of converging (Julia goldens 0.17, 0.19, 0.11, 0.16 at N = 12, 16, 24, 32) and the adaptive reference integrators stall (Tsit5 step limit). fire_behavior is unaffected in practice because ENO1 acts only far from the front and near the walls, where its level set barely moves and is periodically reinitialised. DESIGN CHOICES, each forced by the scheme: (1) g INCREASES TOWARD EVERY WALL, so fire_behavior's guarded extrapolation ghost max(2a - b, a, b) is the plain linear one; on a field that decreases toward a wall the guard clamps the ghost to the inner value and the wall cell's ENO1 derivative vanishes -- an O(1) error the reference code accepts because a fire's level set always grows toward the walls -- which rules out a plane (a manufactured x + y drifts by exactly T sqrt(2) at the corner). Any smooth field increasing toward all four walls has an interior critical point; the paraboloid's is at the centre, where |grad g| = 0 and the forcing has a cone, but the scheme differentiates the smooth COMPONENTS g_x, g_y, so the norm's cone costs no order. (2) weno_band_cells = 1e9: the WENO5 branch covers the whole interior block and the ENO1 branch only the bdy_eno1_cells = 3 wall band, so the WENO reconstruction is gated over the largest possible region (with a finite band on a frozen field the partition would also be static, but the gated region would shrink with h). The band switch itself is exercised value-by-value against fire_behavior's dumps in the EqWeFiC component test and structurally by the AST fixture. (3) The signed distance r - 0.3 is not used: its convex centre kink is not a steady viscosity solution of u_t + S|grad u| = S and fills in. ORDER: the wall band (three cells, plus the guarded-extrapolation wall cells) is first order on a curved field (one-sided minmod, error ~ h g''), the interior WENO5 error is high order once the bump is resolved (N >= 32; ~h^5 away from the bump's critical lines, ~h^3 on them with Jiang-Shu weights); the L2 norm over the box therefore scales as the wall band's share, sqrt(12 N / N^2) x O(h) = O(h^1.5), so the expected L2 order is 1.5 (a NumPy replica of the operator gives 1.43 from N = 16 to 32 and 1.51 from 32 to 64; the Linf error, set by the wall band, is first order and is not gated). The constant wind (1, 1) fixes the WENO upwind side where signo = (grad g . (1,1)) g_x > 0 and flips it in the quadrant where g_x < 0, so both reconstruction orientations are exercised. The reference's L2 norm over the box is ~0.09, so the RELATIVE L2 error (esm-spec 6.6.5) reads about ten times the absolute one; the cap is set from the measured value. COST: the hybrid body evaluates both ifelse branches (seven-point WENO along each axis plus the fourth-order selector) on every interior cell; the accumulator form needs only a handful of right-hand-side evaluations (w is linear in time), which is what makes the sweep affordable. Default resolution NX = NY = 32 (document metaparameters, rebound by the convergence runner). Geometry: x0 = y0 = 0, dx = 1/NX, dy = 1/NY; coordinates spelled inline (build-time scope-free). Per-resolution errors are pinned in tests/conformance/convergence/levelset_weno5_eno1_2d_bump_mms/golden/errors.json.",
    "license": "MIT",
    "tags": [
      "esd:problem",
      "grid:cartesian_uniform_2d",
      "equation:hamilton_jacobi",
      "mms",
      "level_set"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "Munoz-Esparza, D., Kosovic, B., Jimenez, P. A., & Coen, J. L. (2018). An accurate fire-spread algorithm in the Weather Research and Forecasting model using the level-set method. J. Adv. Model. Earth Syst. 10, 908-926.",
        "doi": "10.1002/2017MS001108"
      },
      {
        "citation": "Jiang, G.-S., & Shu, C.-W. (1996). Efficient implementation of weighted ENO schemes. J. Comput. Phys. 126, 202-228.",
        "doi": "10.1006/jcph.1996.0130"
      },
      {
        "citation": "Rouy, E., & Tourin, A. (1992). A viscosity solutions approach to shape-from-shading. SIAM J. Numer. Anal. 29, 867-884.",
        "doi": "10.1137/0729053"
      }
    ]
  },
  "metaparameters": {
    "NX": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 32,
      "description": "Cells along x (default resolution of the inline test; rebound by the convergence runner)."
    },
    "NY": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 32,
      "description": "Cells along y."
    }
  },
  "models": {
    "LevelSetWENO5ENO1BumpMMS": {
      "expression_template_imports": [
        {
          "ref": "../grids/cartesian_uniform_2d/rules/weno5_eno1_norm_D2_extrapolate_bc.esm",
          "bindings": {
            "NX": "NX",
            "NY": "NY"
          }
        }
      ],
      "variables": {
        "u": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m",
          "shape": [
            "x",
            "y"
          ],
          "description": "The manufactured field g = r^2 - 0.09 + 0.05 (max(0, 1 - r^2/0.28^2))^8 as a constant OBSERVED (a bare-LHS equation, not a state), so the hybrid norm is evaluated on the exact field at every time; the truncation error is accumulated in the single state w. (Making g a frozen state alongside w tripped a cell-map ambiguity in the Rust inline-test reduce, which read the first shaped state's cells for w at t > 0.)"
        },
        "S": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "m/s",
          "default": 1.0,
          "description": "Non-negative Hamilton-Jacobi front speed."
        },
        "x0": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "m",
          "default": 0,
          "description": "Left x domain edge (grid free name)."
        },
        "y0": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "m",
          "default": 0,
          "description": "Bottom y domain edge (grid free name)."
        },
        "dx": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m",
          "description": "x-spacing 1/NX: the grid dx free name, NX substituted at load (esm-spec 9.7.6)."
        },
        "dy": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m",
          "description": "y-spacing 1/NY: the grid dy free name, NY substituted at load."
        },
        "wind_u": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m/s",
          "shape": [
            "x",
            "y"
          ],
          "description": "x-component of the advecting wind that biases the WENO5 stencil (rule free name wind_u; fire_behavior uf). Constant field here."
        },
        "wind_v": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m/s",
          "shape": [
            "x",
            "y"
          ],
          "description": "y-component of the advecting wind (rule free name wind_v; fire_behavior vf). Constant field here."
        },
        "bdy_eno1_cells": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 3,
          "description": "ENO1 wall band in cells (rule free name; fire_behavior BDY_ENO1 = 10). The minimum 3 is used so the WENO band exists at every sampled resolution."
        },
        "weno_band_cells": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 1000000000.0,
          "description": "WENO5 band half-width in cells; set to 1e9 so the WENO5 branch is active on the whole interior block and the ENO1 branch only in the three-cell wall band (a static partition: no cell switches branch as u evolves, which keeps the ODE right-hand side continuous in time for the adaptive integrators)."
        },
        "forcing": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "x",
            "y"
          ],
          "description": "The manufactured |grad g| = sqrt(g_x^2 + g_y^2) with g_x = 2 (x - 1/2) - 16 eps (x - 1/2)/w^2 (max(0, 1 - r^2/w^2))^7 and g_y likewise (coordinates spelled inline); it balances the continuous operator so g is the steady exact solution."
        },
        "w": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m",
          "shape": [
            "x",
            "y"
          ],
          "description": "Accumulator state: w(0) = g and w_t = S (|grad g| - |grad u|_h) with u frozen at g, so w(T) - g = -T S (|grad g|_h - |grad g|) is exactly T times the truncation error of the hybrid norm on the manufactured field -- a linear accumulation with no feedback, chosen because the coupled forced-steady form u_t = S (|grad g| - |grad u|_h) is not robustly stable in the ENO1 wall band (the minmod selection is not upwind for the transported error and the adaptive reference integrators stall), see metadata.description."
        }
      },
